'use strict';
const CATEGORIES=['Opening & agenda','Empathy & cues','Explaining & uncertainty','Shared decisions','Medical complexity','Difficult conversations','Safety & follow-up','Ethics & inclusion'];
const PHRASE_ROWS=[
[0,'Open the conversation','A patient arrives with a story you have not yet heard.','What has been happening, and what would you most like us to work on today?','What is the problem? You have ten minutes.','An open invitation gives the patient room to reveal both the symptom and the purpose of the consultation. Pause before moving to focused questions.','Let the opening account finish; then clarify duration, severity and impact.','Invite their story|Ask their priority|Allow a pause'],
[0,'Find the whole agenda','The patient mentions several problems.','Before we decide where to start, is there anything else you hoped we could discuss?','One problem per appointment.','Early agenda mapping reduces late surprises. It does not mean promising to solve every problem in one sitting.','List the concerns, screen urgent symptoms and negotiate a realistic order.','Invite further concerns|Check urgency|Agree an order'],
[0,'Negotiate priorities','Knee pain, repeat medicines and new breathlessness compete for time.','Your knee is really affecting you. I also need to check the new breathlessness first because it could need urgent treatment. Then we can agree a plan for your knee.','We cannot do your knee today.','Explain the reason for prioritising while showing that the patient’s original concern has been heard.','Assess the urgent symptom and arrange a definite review for deferred problems.','Acknowledge impact|Explain the risk priority|Plan the deferred issue'],
[0,'Explore ideas naturally','The patient has searched online and fears a serious diagnosis.','What have you been wondering might be behind this?','What are your ideas, concerns and expectations?','Use everyday language. Explore beliefs when they arise rather than reciting a checklist.','Ask what information shaped the belief and what would help resolve the concern.','Use plain language|Explore their belief|Follow the answer'],
[0,'Uncover the worry','A parent repeatedly asks whether a cough is normal.','You have asked about that a couple of times. Is there something in particular you are worried we might be missing?','I have already told you it is fine.','Repetition may be a cue that the main concern has not been addressed.','Reassess relevant features before offering proportionate reassurance.','Notice the cue|Invite the concern|Avoid premature reassurance'],
[0,'Start safely by phone','You are beginning a remote consultation.','Could I confirm your name and date of birth, where you are now, and a number to call if we get cut off? Are you somewhere you can speak privately?','I assume you are at home.','Identity, location, privacy and a reconnection plan make remote care safer.','Check who else is present, consent to their involvement and whether remote assessment is suitable.','Confirm identity and location|Check privacy|Arrange reconnection'],
[1,'Name the specific burden','A carer says they are awake every night.','You are trying to keep your partner safe and barely getting any sleep yourself. That sounds exhausting.','I understand how you feel.','Reflect the detail you actually heard. Specific empathy demonstrates attention without claiming to know exactly how someone feels.','Explore the carer’s own health, immediate ability to cope and available support.','Reflect a specific detail|Name the emotion tentatively|Explore support'],
[1,'Respond to a quiet cue','The patient says, “There is not much point anymore.”','When you say there is not much point, could you tell me what that means for you? Have you been thinking about ending your life?','Let us focus on your tablets.','A vague statement may hide distress or suicidal thoughts. Clarify directly and calmly.','Assess current thoughts, intent, plans, access to means, recent actions, protective factors and immediate safety.','Explore meaning|Ask directly about suicide|Assess immediate safety'],
[1,'Make room for emotion','A patient starts crying after hearing concerning results.','We can take a moment. I can see this is a lot to take in. What is going through your mind?','Do not cry; it may be nothing.','A pause and an invitation are often more useful than additional information.','Check understanding and support before giving the next small piece of information.','Allow a pause|Acknowledge emotion|Invite their response'],
[1,'Validate without agreeing','The patient believes all medicines are harmful.','It sounds as though your previous reaction has made taking another medicine feel risky. Could we look at that experience and the options together?','Yes, medicines are dangerous.','Validate the experience without endorsing an inaccurate conclusion.','Clarify the previous drug and reaction, record relevant allergy information and compare realistic options.','Validate experience|Explore evidence|Offer collaboration'],
[1,'Address shame','A patient hesitates to discuss alcohol.','Thank you for telling me. My role is to help you work out what support you need, without judging you.','You should know better.','A non-judgemental response makes further disclosure easier.','Assess dependence, withdrawal, physical harm, mood, dependants and practical barriers.','Thank them|Avoid judgement|Move to relevant assessment'],
[1,'Repair a missed cue','You realise you changed topic when the patient mentioned bereavement.','I moved on quite quickly when you mentioned your husband. I am sorry. How has losing him been affecting you?','Anyway, back to your symptoms.','A brief repair restores attention to the person without making the consultation about the clinician.','Allow the patient to choose how much to share and assess functioning and support.','Acknowledge the missed cue|Apologise briefly|Return to their experience'],
[2,'Explain a working diagnosis','Symptoms suggest a likely cause but examination is incomplete.','From what you have described, one possibility is reflux. I cannot confirm that by phone alone, and I want to check the features that could point to something more serious.','It is definitely reflux.','Separate the current hypothesis from what remains unknown. Uncertainty should lead to a clear next step.','Check red flags and arrange examination or escalation appropriate to the presentation.','State a working diagnosis|State the limit|Describe the next step'],
[2,'Explain a test','You are arranging a kidney function blood test.','This blood test checks how your kidneys are working and the balance of salts in your blood. It will help us decide whether your current medicines are safe.','We will do routine U&Es.','Link a test to the decision it will inform. Avoid unexplained abbreviations.','Confirm timing, who will review the result and how the patient will hear from the practice.','Use everyday words|Explain the purpose|Close the results loop'],
[2,'Explain an urgent cancer referral','A concerning symptom requires investigation.','There are several possible causes. Cancer is one we need to check for promptly, so I recommend an urgent specialist assessment. The referral does not mean that you have cancer.','It is just a routine check; nothing to worry about.','Be honest about the reason for urgency without presenting a suspected diagnosis as confirmed.','Explain the next step, track referral receipt and agree what to do if no appointment arrives.','Name the concern honestly|Explain uncertainty|Give a concrete referral plan'],
[2,'Chunk and check','You have explained the first part of a treatment plan.','I have covered why we are considering treatment. Shall I pause there—what questions does that bring up before we discuss the options?','Do you understand everything?','Checking after a small chunk lets the patient reveal misunderstandings before more information is added.','Use their response to adjust the depth and vocabulary of the explanation.','Offer a short chunk|Invite questions|Adapt to the response'],
[2,'Communicate absolute risk','You need to explain a numerical benefit.','Using an illustrative example, if 10 out of 100 people have a problem without treatment and 7 out of 100 with it, that is 3 fewer people out of 100 over the same period.','It reduces your risk by 30%, so you need it.','Use the same denominator and timeframe. These numbers are a teaching example, not a real treatment estimate.','Use a validated decision aid for the actual intervention and discuss uncertainty and harms as well as benefit.','Use absolute numbers|Keep timeframe consistent|Balance benefits and harms'],
[2,'Explain persistent symptoms','Tests are reassuring but symptoms continue.','The results make some serious causes less likely. They do not mean your symptoms are imaginary. Let us review what is still affecting you and what we should do next.','All the tests are normal, so nothing is wrong.','Validation and a continuing plan avoid both dismissal and endless unstructured investigation.','Review the course, examination, differential diagnosis, function and any new warning signs.','Validate symptoms|Explain limits of tests|Agree a review plan'],
[3,'Offer genuine options','There is more than one reasonable management choice.','We have a few reasonable options. I can explain what each might help with, its downsides, and what choosing to wait would involve. Which matters most to you?','You can choose whatever you like.','Shared decisions combine clinical advice with the person’s values; they do not transfer all responsibility to the patient.','Include waiting or no treatment only when clinically appropriate, then make a reasoned recommendation.','Compare reasonable options|Explore priorities|Give clinical guidance'],
[3,'Make a recommendation','The patient asks what you recommend.','Given the symptoms and your kidney condition, I recommend this option because it reduces the risk of harm. How does that fit with what you were hoping for?','It is entirely up to you.','A clear recommendation can support autonomy when the reasoning is transparent and the patient can respond.','Check contraindications, alternatives and consent; adapt if new preferences or facts emerge.','State a recommendation|Explain why|Invite their view'],
[3,'Check feasibility','A plan requires regular appointments during working hours.','How would these appointments fit around your shifts and caring responsibilities? What might make the plan difficult to follow?','You must make time for your health.','A clinically reasonable plan can fail if it does not fit daily life.','Explore access, money, transport, language, dexterity and support; agree practical changes.','Ask about daily life|Explore barriers|Adapt the plan'],
[3,'Elicit change talk','The patient is unsure whether to change a habit.','What do you like about it, and what has started to worry you? If you did make a change, what would you hope to gain?','You need more willpower.','Exploring both sides reduces confrontation and lets the patient articulate their own reasons.','Agree one achievable step if they are ready; assess dependence before advising reductions in alcohol or medicines.','Explore ambivalence|Ask their reasons|Agree a safe small step'],
[3,'Respond to refusal','The patient declines a recommended hospital assessment.','Can you help me understand what is making hospital difficult today? I would like to explain what worries me, then see how we can make the safest plan with you.','If you refuse, it is your responsibility.','Refusal may reflect fear, practical barriers or misunderstanding. Do not equate disagreement with incapacity.','Assess decision-specific capacity when indicated, explain material risks, address barriers, seek senior help and document the discussion.','Explore the reason|Explain material risk|Develop the safest achievable plan'],
[3,'Agree a manageable goal','The patient feels overwhelmed by lifestyle advice.','Of the changes we have discussed, which feels most useful and realistic this week? Let us make it small enough to try and review together.','Lose weight, exercise more and stop drinking.','One meaningful goal is more actionable than a list of demands.','Specify the action, frequency, support and review date; tailor activity to clinical status.','Let them choose|Make the goal specific|Set a review'],
[4,'See the whole person','Several conditions and treatments dominate the record.','Of everything you are dealing with, what is getting in the way of the life you want most?','Which disease shall we deal with first?','Functional goals reveal priorities that disease targets alone may miss.','Link management to goals while checking immediate risks and interactions between conditions.','Ask about life impact|Elicit a goal|Retain safety priorities'],
[4,'Explore treatment burden','The patient has stopped some medicines.','Could you talk me through a usual day with your medicines? Which are hardest to manage, and have any caused problems?','Why are you non-compliant?','Ask about the actual regimen without blame. Cost, side effects and complexity can be more important than knowledge.','Reconcile prescribed, over-the-counter and herbal medicines, indications, doses, allergies and monitoring.','Ask what they actually take|Explore barriers and effects|Reconcile safely'],
[4,'Explain a prescribing trade-off','A requested painkiller may worsen another condition.','I want to relieve your pain. The difficulty is that this tablet can put extra strain on your kidneys, so I would like to discuss options that fit both problems.','You cannot have pain relief because of your kidneys.','Name the competing goals and offer a constructive alternative.','Check renal function, other medicines and non-drug options; tailor analgesia and monitoring.','Acknowledge the goal|Explain the interaction|Offer safer options'],
[4,'Avoid diagnostic overshadowing','An anxious patient reports new chest discomfort.','Anxiety can cause physical symptoms, but I do not want to assume it explains a new symptom. I need to check what is happening and whether you need urgent assessment.','It is your anxiety again.','A previous diagnosis must not prevent assessment of a new problem.','Assess acute features, risk factors and examination needs; escalate time-critical symptoms.','Avoid assumptions|Assess the new symptom|Act on urgency'],
[4,'Coordinate care','Several teams are changing the treatment plan.','Let us agree who is coordinating this. I will clarify the plan with the team, check that our medicine list matches, and arrange a review with you.','The hospital should sort that out.','Ownership reduces gaps at transitions of care.','Send a clear handover, verify receipt when urgent and assign responsibility for monitoring and results.','Name an owner|Reconcile the plan|Arrange continuity'],
[4,'Plan a staged review','There are more needs than can safely be addressed now.','Today we need to deal with the dizziness and make sure your medicines are safe. I will also arrange a longer appointment for the pain and the support you need at home.','We have run out of time.','A staged plan is appropriate when deferred issues are checked for urgency and given an actual route back.','Document unresolved issues and arrange a specific review, rather than simply asking the patient to rebook.','Prioritise explicitly|Acknowledge remaining needs|Book a defined review'],
[5,'Decline an unsafe prescription','A patient drinking heavily requests sleeping tablets.','I can hear how desperate you are for sleep. Adding a sleeping tablet to alcohol could seriously affect your breathing and alertness. I recommend we assess you and make a safer plan for both the sleep and drinking.','No, you are drug seeking.','Explain the patient-specific risk and offer active help. A boundary without an alternative feels like rejection.','Assess current intoxication, withdrawal, self-harm risk and other medicines; arrange appropriate support.','Acknowledge the need|Explain specific harm|Offer an active alternative'],
[5,'Respond to anger','The patient feels a previous clinician ignored them.','You feel that your concerns were not heard, and that has left you angry. I want to understand what happened and make sure we address what you need now.','Calm down.','Name the experience without becoming defensive or promising an outcome before investigation.','Check immediate clinical needs, listen, clarify expectations and offer the complaints process; maintain safe boundaries.','Reflect the experience|Listen without defensiveness|Address current care'],
[5,'Break concerning news','A result may indicate a serious diagnosis.','I am afraid the result has raised a concern that needs further investigation. Would it be okay to talk through what we know so far?','Your results are bad.','A brief warning prepares the patient. Give one clear fact, pause and check their response.','Ensure privacy, offer support, explain uncertainty and arrange the next step.','Prepare the patient|Give a clear small chunk|Pause and plan'],
[5,'Handle repeated reassurance seeking','The patient is frightened despite reassuring assessment.','What is the possibility that feels hardest to put out of your mind? Let us look at what we have checked, what the results can tell us, and what would change the plan.','I promise it is not cancer.','Explore the fear and give bounded reassurance without guarantees or repeated low-value testing.','Review new symptoms, agree a follow-up plan and consider support for persistent health anxiety.','Explore the fear|Avoid guarantees|Agree review triggers'],
[5,'Correct misinformation respectfully','The patient quotes an inaccurate health claim.','I can see why that would be worrying. Would you mind telling me what you heard? Then we can compare it with what is known and discuss your options.','That is nonsense.','Curiosity protects the relationship and clarifies the precise belief to address.','Correct the claim plainly, explain uncertainty and check what the patient takes from the discussion.','Explore the claim|Correct respectfully|Check understanding'],
[5,'Interrupt with a reason','A long account is preventing assessment of a possible emergency.','I am sorry to interrupt. You mentioned chest pain happening now, and I need to ask a few urgent questions so we can get you the right help.','Please get to the point.','A brief interruption is appropriate when its purpose is clear and safety requires it.','Switch to focused assessment and immediate escalation when indicated.','Apologise briefly|Explain the safety reason|Focus the assessment'],
[6,'Give specific safety-netting','A patient is going home after an assessment.','If you develop the warning symptoms we have discussed, seek help through the route we have agreed rather than waiting for our review. Let us go through exactly which symptoms, how urgently, and whom to contact.','Come back if worse.','A safety net needs a trigger, action, urgency and route. Fill in the actual details for this patient; the template is not sufficient on its own.','Use condition-specific warning signs, expected course, review date and a written plan.','Name specific triggers|Give action and urgency|Give an accessible contact route'],
[6,'Use teach-back','You want to check the plan was clear.','I want to check I have explained this clearly. Could you talk me through what you will do when you get home and when you would seek urgent help?','Repeat what I just said.','Make the test about your explanation, not the patient’s intelligence.','Listen for errors, re-explain a smaller chunk and check again.','Own the clarity|Ask for their plan|Correct misunderstandings'],
[6,'Close the results loop','You are arranging investigations.','I will review the results when they arrive and contact you by the time we have agreed. If you have not heard by then, please contact the practice; do not assume silence means the results are normal.','We only call if something is wrong.','Assigning responsibility protects against missed or unreviewed results.','Record the test, expected return, named reviewer, contact method and backup route.','Name a reviewer|Agree a timeframe|Explain the backup plan'],
[6,'Give a clear emergency instruction','Current persistent chest pressure with sweating suggests a possible emergency.','These symptoms could be a heart attack. We need emergency help now. I will arrange an ambulance and confirm your location. Please do not drive yourself.','Book an urgent appointment later today.','When the problem is time-critical, use direct language and act; do not bury the message in a long explanation.','Use the emergency pathway, stay connected where possible and arrange a clear handover.','State urgency|Arrange emergency help|Avoid unsafe transport'],
[6,'Specify follow-up','A new plan needs reassessment.','Let us arrange a review on the agreed date to check how you are feeling, any side effects and whether the plan is helping. Please seek help sooner for the warning symptoms we have discussed.','See how you go.','A review should have a purpose and timing matched to risk, not an arbitrary standard interval.','Book or explicitly delegate the review, record outstanding tests and agree earlier escalation triggers.','Agree timing|State the review purpose|Give earlier escalation advice'],
[6,'Finish with the patient’s agenda','You are closing a consultation.','We have agreed the immediate plan and a review for the remaining concern. Before we finish, what questions or worries have we not addressed?','Everything sorted?','A specific closing check can expose disagreement, confusion or an unresolved concern.','Check feasibility and understanding without reopening a long unfocused history.','Summarise the agreement|Invite remaining concerns|Check feasibility'],
[7,'Explain confidentiality honestly','A young person asks if you will tell their parents.','What you tell me is usually private. If I am worried that you or someone else is at risk of serious harm, I may need to share relevant information to help keep you safe. I would usually discuss that with you first.','I will never tell anyone.','Avoid absolute promises. Confidentiality and safeguarding need a clear, proportionate explanation.','Assess understanding, consent, safety and the relevant UK nation’s framework; consult safeguarding support when needed.','Explain usual privacy|Explain limits|Discuss sharing where safe'],
[7,'Arrange an interpreter','A relative is translating a sensitive consultation.','I want to make sure we understand each other accurately and that you can speak freely. I can arrange a professional interpreter in your preferred language.','Your child can translate.','Professional interpretation supports accuracy, autonomy and privacy.','Check language and dialect, consent, interpreter availability and urgent care needs; speak directly to the patient.','Offer professional interpretation|Respect privacy|Speak to the patient'],
[7,'Assess decision-making support','An adult with a learning disability needs to consider a treatment.','Let us go through this in a way that works for you. What helps you take in information, and is there someone you would like to support you?','Your carer will decide.','A diagnosis does not establish incapacity. Support participation before assessing a particular decision.','Use accessible information, time and communication aids; apply decision-specific capacity assessment when indicated.','Presume capacity|Offer communication support|Include their wishes'],
[7,'Discuss safeguarding','A patient describes coercive behaviour at home.','Is it safe for you to talk now? I am concerned about your safety. We can discuss support, and I will explain what information may need to be shared and why.','Just leave your partner tonight.','Advice must consider immediate danger, safe contact and the person’s circumstances.','Assess children and dependants, immediate threat, safe communication and safeguarding referral with appropriate advice.','Check privacy and safety|Avoid blame or coercion|Agree safe next steps'],
[7,'Explain DNACPR accurately','A family worries that a DNACPR decision means no treatment.','This decision is specifically about whether to attempt CPR if the heart or breathing stops. It does not mean stopping other appropriate treatment or care. Let us discuss the person’s wishes and the wider plan separately.','DNACPR means we will keep them comfortable only.','Separate a CPR decision from the broader treatment plan. Avoid assumptions about what matters to the person.','Use current local policy, involve the patient where possible and document review and communication; legal frameworks vary across the UK.','Separate CPR from other care|Explore wishes|Explain the broader plan'],
[7,'Be open after an error','A patient received an incorrect prescription.','I am sorry this happened. First I want to check whether you have been harmed and make sure your treatment is safe. I will explain what we know, what needs checking, and how we will keep you updated.','I cannot comment because it was another doctor.','An apology and factual explanation are compatible with checking uncertain details. Avoid speculation and defensive language.','Address immediate harm, correct the prescription, escalate safety concerns and follow candour and incident-reporting procedures.','Apologise and explain facts|Address immediate safety|Take ownership of follow-up']
];
const PHRASES=PHRASE_ROWS.map((r,i)=>({id:'p'+(i+1),category:CATEGORIES[r[0]],title:r[1],cue:r[2],say:r[3],avoid:r[4],why:r[5],action:r[6],checks:r[7].split('|')}));
const MODULES=[
{ id:'m1',title:'Build a consultation that flows',category:'Foundations',time:12,desc:'Move from the patient’s story to a shared, workable plan.',objective:'Conduct a focused consultation without sounding like a checklist.',intro:'Good structure should make space for the patient and protect time for a safe management plan. The sequence can change when urgency, emotion or new information demands it. Do not keep taking history simply because more questions are available.',steps:[['Open and orient','Introduce yourself, establish identity and the consultation setting, check privacy and invite the patient’s account. In remote care, know their location and how to reconnect.'],['Map and prioritise','Find the main concern and any additional problems. Identify time-critical symptoms early. Explain why you propose a particular order and agree what can be deferred.'],['Focus and synthesise','Explore discriminating clinical features, relevant background and impact. Summarise the key findings aloud, give a working explanation and invite correction.'],['Manage and close','Make a recommendation, discuss realistic alternatives, check preferences and feasibility, and agree actions, ownership, review and specific safety-netting.']],example:'“You have told me about the pain, the effect on work and your worry about cancer. I would like to explain what I think is happening, what still needs checking, and the options for helping you.”',pitfall:'A complete symptom checklist followed by a rushed list of treatments is not a complete consultation. Integrate the patient’s concern into the explanation and plan.',drill:'In 45 seconds, open a consultation, ask about additional concerns and negotiate a priority. Repeat using different words.',check:['Invited an uninterrupted opening account','Checked urgency before following a routine sequence','Summarised and moved into management','Closed with clear actions and review'],phrases:['p1','p2','p3','p42']},
{ id:'m2',title:'Respond to the person behind the symptom',category:'Communication',time:10,desc:'Hear cues, explore meaning and use specific empathy.',objective:'Respond to emotion in a way that changes the consultation.',intro:'Empathy is an observation followed by a response. “That sounds difficult” is useful when it refers to something specific and is followed by curiosity or support. Avoid repeatedly using stock phrases while continuing your original question list.',steps:[['Notice the cue','Listen for repetition, hesitation, changed tone, silence, unexpected detail or words such as “pointless”. Cues may be emotional, social or clinical.'],['Explore before interpreting','Reflect tentatively: “You sounded worried when you mentioned work.” Ask what the cue means to the patient rather than assuming.'],['Validate the experience','Name the burden without agreeing with an inaccurate belief. Pause if the patient is distressed. Avoid “at least”, guarantees or claims that you know exactly how they feel.'],['Make the response consequential','Use the information: adapt the plan, assess safety, arrange support or revisit the patient’s priority. Acknowledgement alone does not resolve the issue.']],example:'“The pain is one part of this, but it sounds as though being unable to look after your daughter is what worries you most. Have I understood that correctly?”',pitfall:'Do not label a suicidal statement as merely an emotional cue. Explore it directly and assess immediate safety.',drill:'Rewrite “I understand” into three specific reflections: a carer’s exhaustion, a new diagnosis, and worry about missing work.',check:['Used a specific detail','Checked the meaning rather than assuming','Allowed a pause','Changed the assessment or plan in response'],phrases:['p7','p8','p9','p12']},
{ id:'m3',title:'Explain clearly, including what you do not know',category:'Communication',time:12,desc:'Use plain language, uncertainty and teach-back.',objective:'Give an honest explanation that leads to a safe next step.',intro:'An explanation should answer the patient’s question: what might be happening, why you think that, what remains uncertain, and what happens next. Short sentences and concrete comparisons are more useful than technical detail that does not change a decision.',steps:[['Start with their understanding','Ask what they already know or suspect, then connect your explanation to that concern. Ask permission before sharing sensitive or detailed information.'],['Give one useful chunk','Name the working diagnosis in ordinary language. Explain a mechanism only as far as it helps the patient understand the proposed action.'],['Make uncertainty useful','Say what is likely and what cannot yet be excluded. State which assessment, investigation or period of observation will resolve uncertainty, and what would trigger escalation.'],['Check and repair','Invite questions. Use teach-back to check the action plan. If understanding is incomplete, take responsibility and explain a smaller chunk differently.']],example:'“The test suggests anaemia, which means fewer red blood cells carrying oxygen. We need to find the cause as well as treat it. I will explain the next tests and how we will review the results.”',pitfall:'“Normal tests” do not justify “nothing is wrong”. Screening, diagnostic testing and monitoring serve different purposes.',drill:'Explain a blood test in 30 seconds without abbreviations, then add who will review it and when.',check:['Used plain language','Separated evidence from uncertainty','Linked each test to a decision','Checked understanding'],phrases:['p13','p14','p15','p16','p38']},
{ id:'m4',title:'Make shared decisions without losing direction',category:'Management',time:12,desc:'Combine a clear recommendation with the patient’s goals.',objective:'Reach a reasoned decision that the patient can actually carry out.',intro:'Shared decisions do not require presenting every conceivable treatment. Select reasonable options, explain their consequences and help the patient relate them to their own priorities. Some urgent situations leave fewer safe options, but still require explanation and involvement.',steps:[['Clarify the goal','Ask what improvement matters: sleeping, working, walking, staying independent or avoiding a side effect. Distinguish a requested intervention from the need behind it.'],['Compare options fairly','Discuss expected benefits, meaningful harms, treatment burden and uncertainty. Include no treatment or observation when appropriate. For numerical risks, use consistent denominators and timescales.'],['Offer a recommendation','Explain why an option fits the clinical situation. Invite disagreement and address misunderstandings without coercion.'],['Agree implementation','Specify what will happen today, what the patient will do, who will help and when the decision will be reviewed. Check money, transport, access, literacy and caring commitments.']],example:'“You want enough pain relief to get back to walking. Because of your kidney disease, I recommend we avoid that oral anti-inflammatory and look at options with a safer balance for you.”',pitfall:'Asking “What do you want?” after a long unexplained list can leave the patient unsupported. Give professional guidance.',drill:'Compare two reasonable options in 60 seconds, give your recommendation and ask what matters to the patient.',check:['Elicited a meaningful goal','Explained benefits and harms','Made a reasoned recommendation','Checked feasibility'],phrases:['p17','p19','p20','p21','p24']},
{ id:'m5',title:'Manage medical complexity',category:'Complexity',time:16,desc:'Prioritise interacting problems, medicines and real-life barriers.',objective:'Create one coherent plan for a person with several competing needs.',intro:'Complexity is not the length of a problem list. It is the interaction between problems, uncertainty, treatment burden, risk and the person’s circumstances. A condition-specific plan may become unsafe when another illness, medicine or access barrier changes the balance.',steps:[['Identify what cannot wait','Assess acute deterioration and time-critical harm. Do not explain new symptoms solely through an existing chronic or psychiatric diagnosis.'],['Map the interactions','Consider disease–disease, drug–disease and drug–drug effects; frailty, cognition, falls, renal or hepatic impairment; and the burden of appointments and self-management.'],['Choose priorities with the patient','Combine clinical urgency with what matters to them. Separate essential action today from issues for a planned review. Explain why a disease target or drug choice may need individualisation.'],['Make the plan deliverable','Reconcile medicines, reduce avoidable duplication, identify a coordinating clinician and communicate with other teams. Include the person’s support network with appropriate consent.']],example:'“The dizziness could be related to your medicines, but we also need to check other causes. Today I want to assess you and make the immediate plan safe; then we can review the whole treatment burden together.”',pitfall:'Do not solve complexity by automatically stopping all medicines, adding a referral to every service, or treating each diagnosis independently.',drill:'For a patient with pain, kidney disease, falls and caring responsibilities, name the immediate risk, two interactions and one achievable goal.',check:['Prioritised immediate risk','Recognised interactions','Included function and treatment burden','Named responsibility and follow-up'],phrases:['p25','p26','p27','p28','p29','p30']},
{ id:'m6',title:'Prescribe and review medicines safely',category:'Management',time:14,desc:'Turn a prescription into a shared, monitored treatment plan.',objective:'Explain why a medicine is appropriate and how its safety will be checked.',intro:'A medicine decision begins before the name or dose. Establish the indication, the person’s actual regimen, relevant contraindications and their experience of treatment. Use current prescribing references for the specific patient rather than memorised generic doses.',steps:[['Reconcile what is actually taken','Check prescribed medicines, over-the-counter products, supplements, allergies and the nature of previous reactions. Ask about missed doses and reasons without blame.'],['Check suitability','Consider age, pregnancy or breastfeeding, kidney and liver function, interactions, previous response, overdose risk and dependence or withdrawal. Check current product and local prescribing guidance.'],['Explain the practical plan','Describe the intended benefit, how to take it, expected onset, important harms, monitoring and what to do if problems arise. Check the patient’s preference and ability to use the formulation or device.'],['Review and coordinate','Specify tests, timing and the responsible clinician. Changes to long-term medicines may need gradual reduction or specialist coordination. Do not abruptly stop dependence-forming medicines except in exceptional medical circumstances requiring urgent management.']],example:'“Before we change this, could we check what you actually take each day, what helps and what causes problems? Then we can agree a safer plan and how we will monitor it.”',pitfall:'A prescription is not a substitute for assessing a request for sedation, pain relief or withdrawal management. Do not apply a fixed taper to every patient.',drill:'Explain a medication review without using “compliance”. Include benefit, harm, monitoring and follow-up.',check:['Reconciled medicines and reactions','Checked patient-specific risks','Explained use and important harms','Planned monitoring and withdrawal safely'],phrases:['p26','p27','p29','p31']},
{ id:'m7',title:'Handle disagreement and unsafe requests',category:'Difficult conversations',time:14,desc:'Stay compassionate, explain boundaries and offer active help.',objective:'Maintain a therapeutic relationship while making a safe decision.',intro:'A request for a particular treatment often represents a need: sleep, relief, reassurance, recognition or control. Explore that need first. A confident boundary combines acknowledgement, a patient-specific reason and a constructive alternative.',steps:[['Understand the request','Ask what the patient hoped the intervention would achieve and what happened with previous approaches. Avoid labels such as “drug seeking”.'],['Name the relevant risk','Explain the risk in this case, without hiding behind policy or blaming another clinician. Avoid moralising or escalating confrontation.'],['Offer a next step','Propose an assessment, alternative treatment, supported review or specialist input. If declining a requested prescription, still address the symptom and immediate safety.'],['Manage refusal thoughtfully','Explore fear and barriers, support decision-making, explain material risks and assess capacity if indicated. A capable refusal may remain after a good discussion; arrange the safest achievable plan and document it.']],example:'“I want to help with your sleep. I cannot safely add this tablet to the alcohol you have described. Let us assess the shaking today and make a plan that addresses both problems.”',pitfall:'Avoid “your choice, your risk” and promises that an alternative is guaranteed to work. Do not use safeguarding or referral as a threat.',drill:'Practise a 40-second response to “The other doctor always gives it to me”, then respond to “You are not listening”.',check:['Explored the underlying need','Explained a specific risk','Offered active help','Kept language respectful'],phrases:['p23','p31','p32','p35']},
{ id:'m8',title:'Mental health, vulnerability and risk',category:'Complexity',time:16,desc:'Ask directly, respond to risk and build an actionable safety plan.',objective:'Integrate mental state, physical health, context and immediate safety.',intro:'Symptom questionnaires can support assessment and monitoring. They cannot replace a conversation or predict whether an individual will attempt suicide. Assess needs, vulnerabilities and current circumstances, and update the plan as the situation changes.',steps:[['Understand symptoms and context','Explore mood, interest, sleep, appetite, function, stress, substances, prior episodes and treatment. Ask about mania, psychotic symptoms and physical causes when relevant.'],['Ask about safety directly','Ask about suicidal thoughts, intent, plans, access to means, recent actions, past attempts and ability to stay safe. Also consider risk to others, self-neglect and dependants.'],['Match urgency to the situation','An immediate threat to life or serious injury needs emergency care. Significant current risk, psychosis, mania or severe deterioration may require urgent specialist assessment. Do not delay action while completing a questionnaire.'],['Build support and continuity','Agree warning signs, coping steps, people to contact, professional routes and means safety collaboratively. Involve trusted people where appropriate and arrange active follow-up. Do not rely on a promise not to self-harm.']],example:'“I am glad you told me. Are you thinking about ending your life now? Have you made a plan, and do you have access to what you would use? I want us to work out what help you need today.”',pitfall:'A denial of suicidal thoughts or the presence of family support does not, by itself, establish safety. Avoid unsupported low/medium/high predictions.',drill:'Say a direct suicide question aloud, then practise a response to a disclosed plan that moves to immediate action.',check:['Asked directly and calmly','Assessed current intent and access','Included physical and social needs','Matched escalation and follow-up to risk'],phrases:['p8','p11','p37','p43']},
{ id:'m9',title:'Consent, confidentiality and safeguarding',category:'Ethics',time:16,desc:'Support autonomy while recognising when protection is needed.',objective:'Explain privacy and decision-making clearly without absolute promises.',intro:'Ethical complexity often turns on whose decision it is, whether the person can make this decision now, what support they need, and whether there is a serious risk requiring information sharing. UK legal frameworks and local pathways differ; identify the relevant jurisdiction.',steps:[['Hear the patient’s own voice','Offer private conversation, accessible information and a professional interpreter when needed. Do not let a relative’s account automatically replace the patient’s wishes.'],['Support informed decisions','Explain relevant options and consequences. Presume adult capacity; assess a specific decision at a specific time when there is reason for doubt. An unwise decision alone does not show incapacity.'],['Explain confidentiality and limits','Share relevant information with consent where possible. Serious risk or other legal duties may justify or require sharing without consent; use proportionate disclosure and appropriate advice.'],['Act and record','Assess immediate danger, children or dependants, safe contact and the risk of an unsafe message. Use safeguarding leads and emergency pathways when necessary. Record facts, reasoning, decisions and follow-up.']],example:'“I will respect your privacy. I am concerned about what you have told me, and I would like us to discuss how we can get support safely, including what information may need to be shared.”',pitfall:'Never guarantee anonymity, assume a parent or next of kin has automatic authority, or infer incapacity from age or diagnosis.',drill:'Explain confidentiality to a young person in 30 seconds, then adapt it for a carer seeking information about an adult.',check:['Included the patient directly','Supported decision-making','Explained confidentiality limits','Planned proportionate safeguarding action'],phrases:['p43','p44','p45','p46','p47','p48']},
{ id:'m10',title:'Safety-netting that changes what happens',category:'Management',time:12,desc:'Connect warning signs to urgency, action and a reliable route.',objective:'Make deterioration, follow-up and test ownership explicit.',intro:'“Come back if worse” leaves too much interpretation to the patient. Safety-netting should reflect the working diagnosis, what remains uncertain, expected progress and the person’s ability to access help. It complements appropriate management; it does not make an unsafe discharge safe.',steps:[['Explain what to expect','Describe the expected course when reasonably known, and acknowledge uncertainty. Avoid promising recovery by a fixed date without justification.'],['Name meaningful warning signs','Choose specific, relevant symptoms that change the urgency. Distinguish emergency signs from those needing same-day review and those needing planned reassessment.'],['Give a usable action','Specify who to contact, how urgently and an alternative if the usual service is unavailable. In an emergency, initiate help rather than merely giving a number.'],['Close all loops','Agree follow-up and ownership of results and referrals. Use teach-back and written information in an accessible format. Document the actual advice, not just “safety-netted”.']],example:'“If you develop severe breathlessness, chest pain or collapse, call 999. If you are getting worse without those emergency symptoms, seek same-day assessment through the route we have agreed. Do not wait for the routine review.”',pitfall:'A long list of every possible complication may obscure the essential action. Select warnings for this clinical situation.',drill:'Write four lines: expected course; warning symptom; action and timeframe; named review or results owner.',check:['Used relevant specific symptoms','Separated emergency and other urgency','Gave an accessible route','Confirmed understanding and ownership'],phrases:['p37','p38','p39','p40','p41']},
{ id:'m11',title:'Remote consulting and practical barriers',category:'Complexity',time:12,desc:'Know when to change modality and make care accessible.',objective:'Recognise the limits of remote assessment and solve barriers to the plan.',intro:'A good remote consultation is not simply an in-person consultation without examination. The medium changes what can be observed, who may be listening and how help can be arranged. Do not invent normal examination findings or use a video view to exclude serious disease.',steps:[['Establish the setting','Confirm identity, location, callback details, privacy and other participants. Ask whether the person can hear and communicate comfortably.'],['Assess suitability','Identify what the remote encounter cannot establish. Arrange face-to-face assessment or emergency care when needed; do not delay escalation to complete a video review.'],['Adapt communication','Use shorter chunks, explicit pauses and verbal summaries. Arrange interpretation or accessible communication support. Check understanding rather than relying on a nod or silence.'],['Solve the access problem','Explore transport, caring duties, disability, work and digital access. Agree a real route to assessment, with handover and a backup plan if the arrangement fails.']],example:'“I cannot assess the cause of your new dizziness safely by phone alone. I recommend an examination today. You mentioned you cannot leave your partner—let us work out how to get you assessed without leaving them unsupported.”',pitfall:'Do not turn “needs face-to-face” into the entire management plan. Explain urgency, location, arrangements and contingency.',drill:'For an unwell patient who cannot travel, practise explaining why assessment is needed and how you will arrange it.',check:['Checked remote safety basics','Recognised missing assessment','Matched modality to urgency','Resolved practical access'],phrases:['p6','p21','p28','p29']},
{ id:'m12',title:'Turn practice into lasting improvement',category:'Learning method',time:10,desc:'Use retrieval, feedback, spacing and varied application.',objective:'Build adaptable skills through repeated, focused attempts.',intro:'Reading a good phrase makes it familiar; producing it unaided tests whether you can use it. Practise the purpose of the phrase, not an exact script. Transfer improves when you use the same skill with different patients and receive specific feedback.',steps:[['Attempt before revealing','Read the patient’s cue, then say or write your response without looking. Commit to an answer and note your confidence.'],['Compare and diagnose the gap','Use the worked example and checklist. Was the issue wording, missing clinical action, missed emotion or poor prioritisation? Choose one change.'],['Repeat immediately with variation','Try again in your own words, then change the context: phone rather than video, a carer rather than the patient, or an access barrier.'],['Return after a delay','Use the review queue and mix topics. The studio schedules another attempt after 10 minutes, 1 day or 3 days depending on your rating, then lengthens successful reviews. These are practical intervals, not a validated exam prediction.']],example:'“Next time I will name the patient’s specific concern before explaining the risk, then ask which part of the plan feels difficult.”',pitfall:'Hours of passive reading, a remembered script or a high self-rating do not establish clinical competence. Seek observed practice and feedback from your trainer.',drill:'Do one phrase attempt, one case decision and one reflection. Return to the difficult phrase tomorrow and use it in a different case.',check:['Attempted before revealing','Identified one specific gap','Repeated with a changed context','Scheduled a later review'],phrases:['p3','p20','p27','p38']}
];
const CASES=[
{id:'c1',title:'“I just need something to sleep.”',patient:'Martin, 46',mode:'Telephone',category:'Mental health',level:'High complexity',summary:'Insomnia, alcohol dependence, work pressure and an unsafe prescription request.',brief:'A self-employed tradesperson requests sleeping tablets. He has been waking at 3 am for three months, is under financial pressure and drinks most evenings. You have no observations or current examination.',opening:'I cannot carry on with these nights. Can you give me something strong so I can get some sleep?',hidden:[['Drinking and withdrawal','He describes six cans of strong lager most evenings, morning shaking relieved by drinking, and a previous failed attempt to stop. Establish the actual can size and strength, last drink, current symptoms and any past seizures or delirium.'],['Impact and safety','He sometimes drives his van after drinking and operates machinery. He has two children at home. Ask about mood, suicidal thoughts, injuries, self-neglect, family safety and other sedating medicines.'],['What matters','He fears losing his work and being judged. He is willing to be assessed but thinks an alcohol service will force him into hospital.']],choices:[{q:'What is the best immediate direction?',options:['Give a short sleeping-tablet course and review next month.','Acknowledge the sleep problem, assess withdrawal and immediate safety, and arrange appropriate assessment.','Tell him to stop drinking completely tonight and try sleep hygiene.'],answer:1,why:['Alcohol and sedatives can cause serious harm. A short course does not remove that interaction or address dependence.','This keeps his priority in view while identifying the risk that changes today’s plan.','Abrupt cessation or a large reduction can precipitate severe withdrawal in a dependent person.']},{q:'He says, “So you are refusing to help?” Choose the strongest response.',options:['“You need to accept that this is your fault.”','“I will give it if you promise not to drink.”','“I do want to help with sleep. This combination could be dangerous. Let us assess the shaking today and arrange a supported plan for both problems.”'],answer:2,why:['Blame increases shame and reduces disclosure.','A promise does not make prescribing safe or address the withdrawal risk.','This acknowledges the goal, explains the risk and offers active care.']}],management:['Assess current intoxication, withdrawal, previous severe withdrawal, physical illness, recent injuries and immediate mental health risk. Arrange same-day assessment for this presentation; severe withdrawal, seizures or confusion require emergency care.','Explain that sudden stopping or large reductions can be dangerous when dependent. Arrange a medically supported withdrawal assessment; do not provide a generic home detox or alcohol taper.','Avoid adding a hypnotic to ongoing heavy alcohol use. Reconcile medicines, consider nutritional deficiency and need for thiamine within the clinical assessment, and arrange appropriate blood tests and specialist support.','Address unsafe driving and machinery now. Clarify current licensing obligations with the relevant authority; explore alternative transport and work support. Assess dependants and safeguarding.','Agree a named follow-up, communication with alcohol services and support for sleep and stress. Provide specific escalation advice and check understanding.'],model:'“Your sleep matters, and I can see how much pressure you are under. The shaking suggests your body may be dependent on alcohol. Stopping suddenly or adding sleeping tablets could be dangerous. I recommend an assessment today so we can make a supported plan and help you sleep more safely.”',trap:'Do not replace the clinical assessment with a label, a sedative prescription, a drinking diary or an instruction to stop suddenly.',checks:['Assessed withdrawal and immediate risk','Explained sedative risk without judgement','Arranged appropriate urgent assessment','Addressed driving, work and dependants','Provided active support and follow-up'],phrases:['p11','p31','p21']},
{id:'c2',title:'Pain relief with competing risks',patient:'Aisha, 72',mode:'Video',category:'Multimorbidity',level:'High complexity',summary:'Knee osteoarthritis, kidney disease, asthma and a request for ibuprofen.',brief:'Aisha has chronic kidney disease and asthma. Knee pain makes shopping difficult. She asks for stronger ibuprofen because a neighbour finds it helpful. You need to clarify her current medicines and renal function.',opening:'If you could just give me that painkiller, I could get back to looking after myself.',hidden:[['Pain and red flags','Symptoms are longstanding but worsening. Ask about trauma, hot swollen joint, fever, night pain, function and examination findings rather than assuming osteoarthritis accounts for every change.'],['Medicines','She buys ibuprofen and takes a blood-pressure medicine. Establish the full regimen, doses, duration, allergies, prior NSAID-related asthma, kidney function and other relevant risks.'],['Daily life','She wants to walk to the shop and has no transport to physiotherapy. Explore balance, falls, mood, weight where relevant and what support she wants.']],choices:[{q:'Which plan best integrates the competing problems?',options:['Assess the pain and medicine risks, explain the trade-off and agree appropriate drug and non-drug options.','Prescribe oral ibuprofen with a stomach protector; that removes the risk.','Decline all treatment because she has kidney disease.'],answer:0,why:['The goal remains pain relief and function, with treatment tailored to her risks.','Gastroprotection does not remove renal, cardiovascular or bronchospasm risk.','Kidney disease changes the choice and monitoring; it does not remove the need to treat pain.']},{q:'How should you phrase the recommendation?',options:['“You should have looked after your kidneys.”','“I want to help you walk more comfortably. That tablet could harm your kidneys, so let us compare safer options and make the exercises accessible.”','“All painkillers are dangerous for you.”'],answer:1,why:['This is blaming and clinically unhelpful.','This connects her goal, the specific risk and practical alternatives.','This overstates the risk and closes down useful treatment.']}],management:['Assess the pain, function, examination needs and features suggesting another diagnosis or urgent pathology.','Reconcile all medicines and review renal function, asthma history and gastrointestinal and cardiovascular risks. Avoid treating a PPI as protection from all NSAID harms.','Discuss therapeutic exercise and accessible physiotherapy, weight support if appropriate and an individualised analgesic plan. Consider topical treatment suitability and other options using current prescribing guidance.','Explore aids, falls prevention, transport and home support. Set a functional goal chosen by the patient.','Arrange review of benefit and adverse effects, and consider specialist options when symptoms and function remain substantially affected despite appropriate management.'],model:'“Getting to the shop matters to you. I want to improve the pain, but oral ibuprofen could put your kidneys at risk and may affect some people’s asthma. Let us review what you take and agree an approach that helps your knee safely.”',trap:'Do not assume that “topical” means risk-free or that all asthma is an absolute contraindication to every NSAID. Individualise.',checks:['Assessed change in symptoms','Reconciled medicines and renal risk','Explained the trade-off','Included exercise and function','Resolved access and arranged review'],phrases:['p25','p26','p27']},
{id:'c3',title:'“These tablets are making me dizzy.”',patient:'Peter, 81',mode:'Telephone',category:'Multimorbidity',level:'High complexity',summary:'Falls, possible postural hypotension, polypharmacy and living alone.',brief:'Peter has hypertension, type 2 diabetes and several regular medicines. He reports dizziness on standing and a recent fall. A hospital team changed his treatment last week.',opening:'I think I should stop the lot. I cannot risk falling again.',hidden:[['Immediate assessment','Ask about head injury, loss of consciousness, current symptoms, chest pain, palpitations, focal neurological symptoms, bleeding, hydration and glucose where relevant.'],['Transition of care','He has old and new medication boxes and is not sure which he takes. Obtain discharge information and reconcile actual use with records and pharmacy.'],['Home safety','He lives alone and is frightened to walk to the bathroom at night. Assess function, cognition, support and whether he can safely remain at home.']],choices:[{q:'What is the safest first approach?',options:['Tell him to stop every blood-pressure tablet immediately.','Assume postural hypotension and schedule an annual review.','Assess urgent causes and injury, arrange appropriate examination and reconcile the recent medication changes.'],answer:2,why:['Blanket stopping can cause harm and misses other causes.','The recent fall and change in symptoms need timely assessment.','This addresses both acute risk and a plausible medicine-related cause.']},{q:'Which phrase best supports the plan?',options:['“The hospital has made a mistake.”','“The medicines may be contributing, but we need to check other causes too. I will clarify the changes and help you get assessed safely.”','“Just stand up more slowly.”'],answer:1,why:['Do not assign blame before establishing facts.','It communicates uncertainty, ownership and a concrete next step.','Practical advice alone is not adequate assessment of falls and dizziness.']}],management:['Triage for emergency features and injury; organise timely face-to-face assessment with observations, lying/standing blood pressure where appropriate, examination and targeted tests.','Check actual medicines, duplicate supplies, recent changes, renal function and relevant glucose readings. Consider arrhythmia, bleeding, dehydration and other causes.','Adjust medicines according to findings and patient-specific risk, coordinating with the initiating team when needed. Avoid a blanket stop instruction.','Assess immediate home safety, falls risk, cognition, mobility and support; involve the patient in any care arrangements.','Provide a clear reconciled list, named follow-up and instructions for deterioration. Confirm the pharmacy and other teams have the updated plan.'],model:'“I understand why you want to stop them after a fall. Some tablets could be contributing, but dizziness can have other causes. I want to assess you, check exactly what you are taking and make a safe change rather than leave you to sort the boxes out alone.”',trap:'Do not diagnose dementia from medication confusion, or diagnose postural hypotension without appropriate assessment.',checks:['Assessed emergency features and injury','Arranged suitable assessment','Reconciled old and new medicines','Addressed home safety','Coordinated a written plan and review'],phrases:['p26','p29','p30']},
{id:'c4',title:'An urgent referral the patient does not want',patient:'Helen, 59',mode:'Telephone',category:'Women’s health',level:'Moderate complexity',summary:'Postmenopausal bleeding, cancer worry and caring responsibilities.',brief:'Helen reports a small vaginal bleed. Her periods stopped five years ago and she does not use HRT. She asks whether she can wait because she cares for her partner.',opening:'It was only a little spotting. I really cannot manage hospital appointments.',hidden:[['Clarify the symptom','Confirm the source, timing, recurrence, amount, pain, associated symptoms and medicines. Assess heavy bleeding or instability urgently.'],['Fear','Her sister died of cancer. She thinks an urgent referral means the diagnosis is already known.'],['Barrier','There is no one to stay with her partner. Explore safe temporary support and appointment arrangements rather than simply repeating the recommendation.']],choices:[{q:'Which action is most appropriate for unexplained postmenopausal bleeding in this 59-year-old?',options:['Reassure because the bleeding was small.','Recommend a suspected cancer pathway referral and address barriers; do not delay indicated referral for routine tests.','Wait for recurrence before doing anything.'],answer:1,why:['Even a small episode warrants assessment.','Her age and unexplained bleeding meet the referral criterion; the plan must also be feasible.','Waiting for recurrence risks delay.']},{q:'Which explanation is clearest?',options:['“This is almost certainly cancer.”','“I am referring only to reassure you; there is no risk.”','“There are several possible causes, but we need to check for cancer promptly. The referral does not mean you have cancer. Let us work out support for your partner.”'],answer:2,why:['The diagnosis is not established.','This hides the reason for urgency.','This is honest, proportionate and addresses the practical barrier.']}],management:['Assess stability, confirm the bleeding source and relevant history; arrange appropriate examination with consent and chaperone.','Use an urgent suspected cancer pathway for unexplained postmenopausal bleeding in this person aged 55 or over. HRT-related bleeding needs its own assessment pathway; this case is not on HRT.','Explain the reason for referral and uncertainty; check understanding of likely investigation without guaranteeing an exact test or appointment date.','Address caring responsibilities, preferred contact and accessible appointments. Track referral progress and give a defined contact-back plan if no appointment arrives.','Give escalation advice for heavy bleeding, faintness, severe pain or deterioration, and arrange support while awaiting assessment.'],model:'“It may have a non-cancer cause, but bleeding this long after your periods stopped needs prompt checking. I recommend an urgent specialist referral. I hear that leaving your partner is difficult; let us work on that so you can attend.”',trap:'Do not make ultrasound a prerequisite that delays an indicated referral, or apply an endometrial thickness threshold without the clinical context.',checks:['Recognised the referral indication','Explained cancer uncertainty honestly','Addressed caring barriers','Tracked the referral','Provided tailored safety-netting'],phrases:['p15','p21','p23']},
{id:'c5',title:'The inhaler that no longer feels enough',patient:'Daniel, 27',mode:'Video',category:'Respiratory',level:'Moderate complexity',summary:'Frequent reliever use, night waking and fear of steroids.',brief:'Daniel uses a blue reliever inhaler repeatedly and wakes with wheeze. He asks for another inhaler but has stopped his preventer because he fears “steroids”. Assess whether he is acutely unwell before discussing long-term control.',opening:'The blue one works. I do not want steroids changing my body.',hidden:[['Current severity','Ask about breathlessness now, ability to speak, response to reliever and other attack features. Arrange urgent or emergency assessment if indicated; do not rely on video appearance alone.'],['Actual treatment','Clarify diagnosis, inhalers, dose, adherence, technique, triggers, smoking/vaping and previous attacks or admissions.'],['Belief','He confuses inhaled corticosteroids with anabolic steroids. Explain their role and discuss side effects without saying there are none.']],choices:[{q:'What should come before a routine step-up discussion?',options:['Check for an acute attack and assess treatment use, technique and beliefs.','Issue more SABA alone because it provides relief.','Tell him inhaled steroids have no adverse effects.'],answer:0,why:['Severity determines urgency; technique and barriers can explain poor control.','SABA-only asthma treatment is not recommended and overuse is a risk signal.','Absolute reassurance is inaccurate and undermines informed choice.']},{q:'Which explanation is most useful?',options:['“If you will not take it, I cannot help.”','“The reliever opens the airways, but inflammation also needs treating. Let us discuss an inhaler plan that treats that and show you how to use it.”','“Double every inhaler whenever you feel breathless.”'],answer:1,why:['A threat does not address the belief or safe care.','It explains the treatment purpose and leads to an individualised plan.','A generic dosing instruction is unsafe across different devices and regimens.']}],management:['Assess for an acute exacerbation and escalate according to severity; long-term control advice must not delay urgent treatment.','Review diagnosis, technique, actual use, triggers, adherence and exacerbation history. Demonstrate and recheck device technique.','Use an ICS-containing strategy. For people aged 12 and over, current pathways include ICS/formoterol AIR or MART according to control and circumstances; check product licensing, dose and local formulary.','Explain the difference between reliever effects and inflammation treatment, discuss relevant adverse effects and address the patient’s steroid concern.','Provide a personalised written action plan with device-specific instructions, emergency triggers and review after treatment change, usually 8–12 weeks for chronic management and earlier when clinically needed.'],model:'“The blue inhaler can ease tightness, but needing it this often tells us the asthma needs reviewing. First I want to check you are safe today. Then we can choose an inhaler plan that also treats the inflammation and that you feel comfortable using.”',trap:'Do not substitute “add montelukast” for reviewing severity, diagnosis, technique and an appropriate ICS-containing regimen.',checks:['Checked acute severity first','Reviewed technique and adherence','Explained an ICS-containing plan','Addressed the specific steroid concern','Agreed an action plan and review'],phrases:['p4','p16','p20']},
{id:'c6',title:'“Everyone would be better off.”',patient:'Leah, 23',mode:'Telephone',category:'Mental health',level:'High complexity',summary:'Low mood, a suicidal cue and immediate safety over routine scoring.',brief:'Leah contacts you about poor sleep and low mood after a relationship ended. She lives alone. During the call she says everyone would be better off without her.',opening:'I am just tired of being a burden. I do not know how long I can keep doing this.',hidden:[['Direct safety questions','She has thought about taking tablets and has access to a supply. Establish current intent, timing, preparations, any ingestion, previous attempts and ability to remain safe.'],['Support and environment','Her sister lives nearby, but Leah has not told her. Confirm location and callback details; discuss involving support where appropriate.'],['Clinical picture','Assess depression, agitation, substance use, psychosis, mania, physical illness and self-care. Do not let a questionnaire delay immediate care.']],choices:[{q:'What is the most appropriate next response?',options:['Complete PHQ-9 before discussing the statement.','Ask directly about suicide, current intent, access to means and any action already taken.','Tell her she has a lot to live for.'],answer:1,why:['A questionnaire does not establish immediate safety.','Direct assessment is necessary to determine the immediate response.','This may feel dismissive and does not assess risk.']},{q:'She says she intends to act tonight and cannot stay safe. What now?',options:['Ask for a promise not to act and review next week.','Email self-help material and close the call.','Arrange immediate emergency/crisis help appropriate to the situation, maintain contact where possible and involve support safely.'],answer:2,why:['A promise is not a safety intervention.','Self-help material is not sufficient for immediate danger.','Act on current danger, confirm location and arrange a clear handover; any suspected overdose needs emergency medical care.']}],management:['Establish immediate safety, location, current intent, plan, access, preparations and any ingestion or injury. Arrange emergency care for imminent danger or overdose; do not leave the person to navigate this alone.','Maintain contact where possible while arranging help. Discuss trusted support and collaborative reduction of access to means without putting others at risk.','Assess wider needs and mental state, including substance use, psychosis, mania and self-neglect. Avoid predictive risk labels based on scales.','Document the actual assessment, clinical reasoning, actions, communication and handover. Confidentiality may need proportionate disclosure to prevent serious harm.','After acute care, ensure active follow-up and a collaborative safety plan. When an antidepressant is started, usual first review is within two weeks, or one week for ages 18–25 or particular suicide concern; immediate risk requires earlier action.'],model:'“I am glad you told me. Are you thinking about ending your life now, and have you taken anything or made a plan? I want to stay with you on the phone while we arrange the help you need to keep you safe.”',trap:'Protective factors and a questionnaire score cannot cancel a stated intent to act. A safety plan is not a no-suicide contract.',checks:['Asked directly about suicide and ingestion','Confirmed location and immediate safety','Acted on imminent risk','Involved support and handed over safely','Arranged continuity after acute care'],phrases:['p8','p43','p29']},
{id:'c7',title:'A parent asks for antibiotics',patient:'Sam, parent of a 4-year-old',mode:'Telephone',category:'Paediatrics',level:'Moderate complexity',summary:'Fever, parental worry, remote limitations and a treatment expectation.',brief:'A parent requests antibiotics for a child with fever and cough. You have not seen the child and do not have reliable observations. The parent is worried because a cousin was admitted with pneumonia.',opening:'Last time we waited and things got worse. Can you just give antibiotics this time?',hidden:[['Assess the child','Clarify age, duration, breathing, responsiveness, hydration, urine output, rash, pain, relevant history and parental concern. Establish whether immediate or same-day assessment is needed.'],['The expectation','The request reflects fear of missing serious illness rather than a preference for antibiotics alone.'],['Access','The parent has no car and another child at home. Work out the route to assessment and escalation rather than giving vague attendance advice.']],choices:[{q:'What is the best first step?',options:['Explain antibiotic resistance and end the call.','Assess illness severity and the parent’s concern, then decide the appropriate examination and urgency.','Prescribe remotely to avoid inconvenience.'],answer:1,why:['Stewardship is important but cannot replace assessment of an unwell child.','The child’s condition determines the plan; the family’s experience shapes the explanation.','Convenience alone does not justify an unassessed prescription.']},{q:'Which response addresses both worry and safety?',options:['“I can hear why that experience worries you. I want to check how your child is now and arrange an examination if needed, so we choose the right treatment.”','“Parents always ask for antibiotics.”','“Coughs are always viral.”'],answer:0,why:['It validates the concern and links assessment to treatment.','This is dismissive.','The claim is false and may miss serious illness.']}],management:['Use an age-appropriate fever assessment and act on emergency features such as severe breathing difficulty, markedly reduced responsiveness or other signs of serious illness.','Arrange examination and observations when needed; remote reassurance must reflect what can actually be established. In a baby under 3 months, temperature 38°C or above is a high-risk feature requiring urgent assessment; this child is 4.','Explain the working assessment and whether antibiotics are indicated. Avoid assuming all coughs are viral or promising antibiotics will prevent deterioration.','Discuss comfort measures, hydration and age-appropriate medicine advice using current guidance; avoid unsafe generic dosing.','Give relevant warning signs, expected course, route to help and review. Check the parent can access the plan and use teach-back.'],model:'“You are worried we could miss something serious, especially after what happened in your family. Let us check how your child is breathing, drinking and responding now, and arrange the right assessment before deciding on antibiotics.”',trap:'Do not use “no fever now” or temporary improvement after antipyretic as proof that serious illness is excluded.',checks:['Assessed age-specific severity','Explored the parent’s concern','Recognised remote limitations','Explained the treatment decision','Gave an accessible safety plan'],phrases:['p5','p6','p38']},
{id:'c8',title:'A private conversation that is not private',patient:'Maya, 34',mode:'Video',category:'Safeguarding',level:'High complexity',summary:'Possible domestic abuse, a partner nearby and unsafe contact risks.',brief:'Maya reports anxiety. She repeatedly looks away from the screen and says her partner handles her money and appointments. You hear someone in the room.',opening:'It is probably nothing. I should not have booked this.',hidden:[['Privacy','Her partner is within earshot. Do not ask detailed abuse questions until you have established a safe way to communicate.'],['Safety','In a safe setting she describes intimidation and financial control. Explore immediate danger, physical or sexual harm, children and dependants, and safe support.'],['Contact','Her partner reads messages on her phone. A standard text reminder or visible information leaflet could increase risk.']],choices:[{q:'What is the best immediate move?',options:['Ask the partner to explain what has been happening.','Ask detailed abuse questions loudly and directly now.','Check whether it is safe to speak and establish a safer method or setting.'],answer:2,why:['This may silence the patient and increase risk.','Sensitive disclosure may be unsafe while the partner can hear.','Privacy and safe contact determine what can safely happen next.']},{q:'Which plan is most appropriate after a disclosure?',options:['Tell her to leave tonight regardless of circumstances.','Assess immediate risk, agree safe support and contact, and use safeguarding pathways proportionately.','Promise never to share anything with anyone.'],answer:1,why:['Leaving can increase danger; planning must be individualised.','This respects autonomy while responding to risk and duties to protect.','Absolute secrecy cannot be promised.']}],management:['Establish privacy and safe communication. If it is unsafe to talk, use a neutral, appropriate way to arrange another contact or immediate help if required.','Assess immediate danger and emergency needs, children/dependants and relevant vulnerabilities. Do not assume the absence of visible injuries means low risk.','Explain confidentiality and possible information sharing; seek safeguarding advice and follow local pathways. Use consent where possible without letting it prevent necessary action for serious risk.','Discuss specialist domestic abuse support and practical options without coercion. Agree safe phone numbers, times, messages and records handling within policy.','Arrange follow-up and document objectively, including the patient’s words, risk assessment and rationale.'],model:'“Before we go further, is it safe for you to speak freely? We can find another way to talk if it is not. You do not deserve to be frightened or controlled, and I want us to plan support in a way that keeps you safe.”',trap:'Do not send revealing texts or leave voicemails without checking safety. Do not make an unsafe separation plan on the patient’s behalf.',checks:['Checked privacy before detailed questions','Assessed immediate danger and dependants','Explained confidentiality limits','Agreed safe contact and support','Documented and arranged follow-up'],phrases:['p6','p43','p46']},
{id:'c9',title:'“Does this mean you are giving up?”',patient:'Robert, 76, with his daughter',mode:'Video',category:'Palliative care',level:'High complexity',summary:'Advanced illness, DNACPR misunderstanding and differing family views.',brief:'Robert has advanced lung disease. His daughter asks about a DNACPR recommendation on a discharge letter. Robert is present but has not yet been invited to speak.',opening:'If his heart stops, surely you have to try everything. Are you stopping his treatment?',hidden:[['The patient’s voice','Robert wants comfort, time at home and honest discussion. Do not assume his daughter speaks for him or that he lacks capacity because he is breathless.'],['Understanding','The family believes DNACPR means no oxygen, antibiotics or hospital care. Clarify the distinction.'],['Wider planning','There is no clear plan for worsening breathlessness, out-of-hours contact or carer support.']],choices:[{q:'How should you begin?',options:['Ask Robert what he understands and wants, with communication support and consent to family involvement.','Discuss only with his daughter because she is next of kin.','Say DNACPR means no further active treatment.'],answer:0,why:['The patient’s wishes and decision-making participation are central.','Next of kin does not automatically confer decision-making authority.','A CPR decision is separate from other appropriate treatment.']},{q:'Which explanation is most accurate?',options:['“The form makes every future decision for us.”','“This concerns CPR if the heart or breathing stops. We need to discuss other treatments and the plan for deterioration separately.”','“It is only valid if every relative agrees.”'],answer:1,why:['It does not define all future treatment.','This corrects the misunderstanding and opens the wider care plan.','Family views matter, but agreement by every relative is not a universal legal requirement.']}],management:['Check Robert’s current symptoms and immediate needs. Establish his understanding, wishes, capacity for relevant decisions and desired family involvement.','Explain CPR in context, likely benefits and burdens based on his clinical situation, and the distinction between DNACPR and broader treatment decisions. Avoid invented success percentages.','Clarify the status of existing documentation and use the local resuscitation and advance-care planning process. A valid applicable advance refusal of treatment has different legal status from a DNACPR clinical recommendation.','Agree symptom support, escalation preferences, coordination with respiratory and palliative teams, carer needs and accessible out-of-hours routes.','Document and share the plan appropriately, arrange review and explain that preferences and clinical circumstances can change. Legal arrangements differ across UK nations.'],model:'“Robert, I would like to hear what matters to you. This recommendation is about CPR, not about stopping all treatment. We should also agree how to help your breathing, what to do if things worsen and how to support you and your daughter.”',trap:'Do not equate DNACPR with “comfort care only”, or assume a family member has legal authority simply because they are next of kin.',checks:['Included the patient directly','Clarified the CPR distinction','Discussed benefits and burdens honestly','Planned symptoms and deterioration','Coordinated and documented review'],phrases:['p25','p45','p47']},
{id:'c10',title:'A mistake, an apology and immediate care',patient:'Nadia, 40',mode:'Telephone',category:'Professional practice',level:'High complexity',summary:'An incorrect prescription and concern about a colleague’s fitness to work.',brief:'Nadia says she was prescribed a drug despite a recorded allergy. The pharmacist stopped it being dispensed. She also reports that the clinician appeared impaired. She remains unwell and wants an explanation.',opening:'I could have been seriously hurt. How can I trust this practice?',hidden:[['Current harm','Confirm whether any doses were taken, any reaction and whether the original illness is still untreated. Acute allergic symptoms may need emergency care.'],['Facts and uncertainty','The patient reports observations; do not confirm intoxication or negligence without evidence. Document the account accurately.'],['Safety concern','If there is a credible immediate risk to other patients, action cannot depend on Nadia submitting a formal complaint.']],choices:[{q:'Which approach best addresses the situation?',options:['Wait for a formal complaint before taking any action.','Apologise, assess current safety, establish facts and escalate credible immediate safety concerns promptly.','Promise the colleague will be dismissed.'],answer:1,why:['A possible current safety risk requires action independently of the complaints process.','It combines care, candour and a proportionate response.','That is unsupported and beyond the clinician’s authority.']},{q:'Which wording is strongest?',options:['“I am sorry this happened. First let us make sure you are safe and have the right treatment. I will raise the safety concern and explain how we will keep you informed.”','“It is not my fault.”','“I guarantee your identity will never be known.”'],answer:0,why:['This is factual, caring and takes ownership.','Defensiveness does not address harm or trust.','Absolute anonymity may not be possible; discuss limits honestly.']}],management:['Assess any exposure, allergic reaction and untreated illness. Arrange urgent treatment or emergency help when indicated; verify and correct the prescription and allergy record.','Apologise, explain established facts and acknowledge uncertainty. Do not speculate about the colleague’s diagnosis, motives or disciplinary outcome.','Escalate credible immediate risk through the clinical lead or appropriate senior route without waiting for a complaint; follow professional patient-safety guidance.','Explain the complaints process separately, possible limits to anonymity and how updates will be provided. Follow candour and incident-reporting duties.','Arrange clinical follow-up and ensure the incident leads to appropriate review, communication and preventive action.'],model:'“I am sorry you have had this experience. I can understand why your trust has been affected. First I want to check you are safe and receiving the right treatment. I will also raise the safety concern promptly and make sure you know what happens next.”',trap:'Do not make promises about confidentiality or disciplinary outcomes that you cannot keep, and do not treat a complaint form as the safety intervention.',checks:['Assessed immediate harm and treatment','Apologised without speculation','Escalated current safety concerns','Explained complaints and limits honestly','Took ownership of follow-up'],phrases:['p32','p48','p29']},
{id:'c11',title:'New symptoms behind an anxiety label',patient:'Chris, 51',mode:'Telephone',category:'Acute care',level:'High complexity',summary:'Persistent chest pressure, sweating and pressure to continue a routine consultation.',brief:'Chris has a history of panic attacks. During a medication review he mentions heavy central chest discomfort happening now, with sweating. He thinks it is another panic attack.',opening:'I do not want to waste anyone’s time. It is probably nerves again.',hidden:[['Time-critical symptoms','Clarify onset and current symptoms rapidly, including breathlessness, radiation, collapse and relevant history, without delaying emergency action.'],['Location','He is alone at a workshop and planned to drive to the surgery. Confirm exact location and callback details.'],['Fear','He worries about leaving the business unattended. Acknowledge this while clearly explaining the urgency.']],choices:[{q:'What is the best next action?',options:['Arrange routine blood tests for anxiety.','Reassure because he has had panic attacks before.','Treat this as a possible cardiac emergency and arrange ambulance assessment now.'],answer:2,why:['Routine tests would delay time-critical care.','A previous anxiety diagnosis does not exclude acute coronary syndrome.','Current persistent chest pressure with sweating warrants emergency assessment.']},{q:'Which instruction is clearest?',options:['“Drive over if you are worried.”','“These symptoms could be a heart attack. We need an ambulance now. Please do not drive; I will confirm your location and arrange help.”','“See whether it settles overnight.”'],answer:1,why:['Self-driving is unsafe in a possible cardiac emergency.','It states the risk and immediate action clearly.','Waiting risks serious harm.']}],management:['Recognise the emergency pattern and initiate emergency assessment immediately. Keep questions focused on what changes immediate action.','Confirm location, callback and whether someone is nearby. Stay connected where possible while help is arranged and use a clear handover.','Tell the patient not to drive. Address practical concerns without allowing them to delay emergency care.','Use current emergency protocols for any immediate treatment after checking relevant contraindications; this practice exercise does not provide a generic drug regimen.','Document assessment, timing, advice and handover. Arrange appropriate continuity after the acute episode.'],model:'“I know you have had panic attacks, but I cannot safely assume that explains this pain. The pressure and sweating could be a heart problem. We need emergency help now, and you should not drive yourself.”',trap:'Do not use safety-netting as a substitute for escalation that is already indicated.',checks:['Avoided diagnostic overshadowing','Recognised time-critical features','Arranged emergency help','Prevented unsafe self-transport','Confirmed location and handover'],phrases:['p28','p36','p40']},
{id:'c12',title:'“My daughter needs you to decide.”',patient:'Grace, 19, with her mother',mode:'Video',category:'Ethics & inclusion',level:'High complexity',summary:'Learning disability, heavy periods, contraception and supported decision-making.',brief:'Grace has a learning disability. Her mother requests an injection to stop periods and prevent pregnancy. Grace is present, but her mother answers every question.',opening:'It would be much easier if you just gave her the injection, doctor.',hidden:[['Grace’s view','Invite Grace to speak using accessible language. She may have preferences about bleeding, pain, treatment and who is present.'],['Clinical assessment','Clarify bleeding severity, anaemia symptoms, pain, possible pregnancy, sexual activity and safety sensitively. Do not infer sexual history from disability.'],['Decision support','Use accessible information, time and communication aids. Assess capacity for the particular decision only when indicated; treatment and contraception may involve different decisions.']],choices:[{q:'What should guide the consultation?',options:['The mother’s preference automatically takes priority.','Speak with Grace, provide support and assess the clinical issue and her own wishes.','Assume Grace cannot consent because she has a learning disability.'],answer:1,why:['A parent does not automatically decide for an adult.','Supported participation is the starting point.','Diagnosis alone does not establish incapacity.']},{q:'Which phrase is most appropriate?',options:['“Grace, what is most difficult about your periods? I can explain the choices in a way that works for you, and you can tell me who you would like to help.”','“Your mother knows best.”','“The injection is the simplest, so we will do that.”'],answer:0,why:['It includes her voice and offers practical support.','It bypasses her autonomy.','Convenience does not replace assessment, consent and informed choice.']}],management:['Assess heavy bleeding, anaemia, pain and other relevant causes; arrange examination or tests according to findings and urgency.','Include Grace directly, check privacy and consent to family involvement, and offer accessible information or communication support.','Discuss appropriate bleeding-management and contraception options separately, including benefits, harms and suitability. Avoid a default treatment based on a carer’s convenience.','Assess decision-specific capacity if necessary using the applicable legal framework. If capacity is lacking, follow the appropriate decision process and include her wishes; safeguarding concerns need their own assessment.','Agree follow-up, how adverse effects or difficulties will be communicated and what support will make the plan feasible.'],model:'“I can see you want to make things easier for Grace. Grace, I would like to hear what you want as well. Let us first understand the bleeding, then explain the options in a way that helps you make the decision.”',trap:'Do not conflate capacity for contraception, consent to examination and consent to sexual activity. Seek specialist or safeguarding advice where needed.',checks:['Heard Grace directly','Assessed the bleeding clinically','Supported informed choice','Avoided assumptions about capacity','Addressed privacy, safety and follow-up'],phrases:['p45','p43','p19']},
{id:'c13',title:'A long-term tablet that cannot simply stop',patient:'Owen, 58',mode:'Telephone',category:'Medicines',level:'High complexity',summary:'Long-term diazepam use, early refill request and withdrawal concerns.',brief:'Owen requests more diazepam after using extra tablets during family stress. He has taken it regularly for years and fears being cut off. You need to verify his regimen and current supply.',opening:'Please do not just stop them. I cannot cope without them.',hidden:[['Immediate risk','Assess current dose, last dose, remaining supply, withdrawal symptoms, sedation, alcohol or other drugs, overdose risk and suicidal thoughts.'],['Treatment history','Clarify the original indication, benefit, side effects, previous withdrawal attempts and existing prescriber plan.'],['Relationship','He feels previous clinicians called him an addict. Use non-stigmatising language and distinguish physical dependence from other patterns of harmful use.']],choices:[{q:'What is the best overall approach?',options:['Stop all prescribing immediately as a punishment for early use.','Agree unlimited extra tablets to maintain trust.','Assess immediate risk, verify treatment and agree a safe, coordinated review and individualised withdrawal plan when appropriate.'],answer:2,why:['Abrupt stopping can cause serious withdrawal and punishment undermines care.','Escalation without assessment can increase harm.','This avoids both abandonment and unsafe automatic continuation.']},{q:'Which response best preserves trust?',options:['“I will not leave you without a plan. We need to check what you have taken and the risks today, then agree how to manage this safely.”','“You are dependent, so you cannot make decisions.”','“Everyone can stop over one week.”'],answer:0,why:['It commits to care while keeping assessment and safety central.','Dependence does not automatically remove capacity.','Withdrawal must be individualised, often with small, gradual reductions.']}],management:['Assess intoxication, withdrawal, co-use, current supply and immediate mental or physical risk. Severe symptoms require urgent or emergency care.','Verify prescribing history and actual use, coordinate with the usual prescriber and pharmacy and avoid duplicate or conflicting plans.','Explain benefits and harms without blame. Do not abruptly stop a dependence-forming medicine except in exceptional medical circumstances requiring urgent management.','If reducing, use a shared, individualised gradual plan with monitoring and adjustment to symptoms; do not supply a generic fixed schedule in this exercise. Address the underlying anxiety or insomnia.','Agree follow-up, one clear prescribing plan, safe storage and what to do if symptoms worsen or supply problems arise.'],model:'“I can hear that you are frightened of being left without support. I will not leave you without a plan. First we need to check what you have taken and how you are feeling, then agree a safe way forward with your regular prescriber.”',trap:'Do not confuse a safe prescribing boundary with sudden withdrawal or abandonment.',checks:['Assessed current use and risk','Verified the prescribing plan','Avoided abrupt cessation','Explained an individualised approach','Arranged coordination and monitoring'],phrases:['p26','p31','p29']},
{id:'c14',title:'Back pain with a new warning symptom',patient:'Alex, 43',mode:'Telephone',category:'Acute care',level:'High complexity',summary:'Back pain, urinary difficulty and anchoring on a familiar diagnosis.',brief:'Alex hurt their back lifting a box. They now describe difficulty starting urine and a new numb feeling around the groin. They ask for stronger painkillers.',opening:'It is probably just the same back strain, but going to the toilet feels different.',hidden:[['Neurological symptoms','Clarify new bladder, bowel or sexual dysfunction, saddle/perineal sensory change, leg weakness and bilateral symptoms. Do not delay urgent action to complete every routine question.'],['Function','They can still walk and think this excludes anything serious. Explain that preserved walking does not rule out a serious nerve problem.'],['Logistics','They plan to wait until after a work shift. Arrange an emergency route and explain why delay is unsafe.']],choices:[{q:'What should the new symptoms trigger?',options:['Treat as a possible cauda equina syndrome and arrange immediate emergency assessment.','Assume urinary difficulty is unrelated and review in two weeks.','Offer a painkiller first and see if the urinary symptom resolves.'],answer:0,why:['New bladder dysfunction and saddle sensory change with back pain require emergency assessment.','Separating the symptoms may miss a time-critical neurological emergency.','Analgesia must not delay the emergency pathway.']},{q:'Which explanation is most appropriate?',options:['“You can walk, so it cannot be serious.”','“The change in bladder function and numbness could mean pressure on important nerves. You need emergency assessment now; we should not wait for your shift to finish.”','“You definitely need surgery.”'],answer:1,why:['Walking does not exclude the condition.','It explains the concern and urgency without claiming a confirmed diagnosis.','The diagnosis and treatment need specialist assessment.']}],management:['Recognise a possible time-critical spinal emergency and arrange immediate assessment through the local emergency pathway.','Clarify essential neurological and systemic features without delaying referral. Do not rely on absence of one symptom or ability to walk for reassurance.','Explain the possibility of nerve compression and the need for urgent examination and investigation; avoid promising or predicting surgery.','Arrange safe transport and handover appropriate to symptoms and local pathway, and confirm the patient understands not to wait.','Document symptoms, onset, advice, escalation and communication. Pain management can accompany but must not replace emergency assessment.'],model:'“This may be more than a strain. New difficulty passing urine with numbness around the groin can mean pressure on nerves that need urgent treatment. I recommend emergency assessment now and will help arrange the next step.”',trap:'Do not explain new urinary symptoms as a prostate problem without considering the back-pain emergency.',checks:['Linked neurological symptoms to back pain','Recognised immediate urgency','Avoided false reassurance','Arranged safe escalation','Documented and handed over'],phrases:['p13','p36','p40']},
{id:'c15',title:'A referral is only useful if it happens',patient:'Farah, 64',mode:'Telephone',category:'Continuity',level:'Moderate complexity',summary:'Missed follow-up, language barriers and a test result that needs ownership.',brief:'Farah has an abnormal blood result and missed two appointments. A relative says she is “not interested”. You have not yet spoken to her with a professional interpreter.',opening:'I received messages, but I did not understand whether I needed to do anything.',hidden:[['Communication','Her preferred language is not English and the written messages were inaccessible. Clarify language, dialect and preferred contact.'],['Clinical urgency','Review the actual result and associated symptoms to determine urgency; “abnormal” alone is not a clinical triage category.'],['Access','She depends on a neighbour for transport and cannot attend the offered times. Explore options rather than labelling her non-attending behaviour.']],choices:[{q:'What is the best next step?',options:['Discharge from follow-up after two missed appointments.','Establish accessible communication, assess result-specific urgency and agree an achievable plan.','Ask a young family member to translate all sensitive information.'],answer:1,why:['Missed appointments may reflect barriers and do not remove responsibility for significant results.','This links clinical risk with the reason the plan failed.','Professional interpretation is preferable for accuracy, privacy and sensitive care.']},{q:'Which wording demonstrates ownership?',options:['“We sent a text, so it is your responsibility.”','“It sounds as though our messages were not clear for you. I will arrange an interpreter and agree how we will contact you about the result and next steps.”','“Your relative can decide whether you attend.”'],answer:1,why:['This shifts responsibility without resolving the safety gap.','It repairs communication and creates a closed-loop plan.','The patient’s own understanding and choice are central.']}],management:['Review the actual result, trends and symptoms and determine whether emergency, same-day or planned action is needed.','Arrange professional interpretation and confirm identity, privacy and consent to any family involvement.','Explain the result, uncertainty and proposed next step in small chunks; use teach-back through the interpreter.','Resolve transport, appointment timing and accessible written information. Name the clinician responsible for reviewing tests or referrals and confirming action.','Document communication needs and the agreed contact/backup route; actively follow up significant unresolved findings.'],model:'“It sounds as though the way we contacted you did not make the next step clear. I would like to arrange an interpreter, explain what the result means and agree a plan that you can attend. We will also agree who will contact you and when.”',trap:'Do not equate missed appointments with informed refusal, or assume a relative’s summary reflects the patient’s wishes.',checks:['Triaged the actual result','Used accessible communication','Explained and checked understanding','Addressed attendance barriers','Named a results and referral owner'],phrases:['p14','p39','p44']},
{id:'c16',title:'When the carer is no longer coping',patient:'Jean, 68',mode:'Video',category:'Palliative care',level:'High complexity',summary:'Carer exhaustion, advanced illness, medication worries and unmet support needs.',brief:'Jean cares for her husband with advanced cancer. She asks for more medication because he is restless at night. She is exhausted and says she cannot manage another night.',opening:'I promised I would keep him at home, but I cannot do this anymore.',hidden:[['Immediate clinical problem','Clarify the patient’s symptoms, onset, pain, breathlessness, confusion, fever, urinary or bowel symptoms and current medicines. Do not assume restlessness is simply insomnia.'],['Carer safety','Ask about Jean’s sleep, health, ability to provide care and available support. Clarify whether the patient can safely remain at home tonight.'],['Goals and support','Explore the patient’s wishes and capacity, existing palliative plan, emergency contacts and services already involved. A previous wish to remain home does not require unsafe care.']],choices:[{q:'What is the most appropriate direction?',options:['Add a sedative without assessing the restlessness.','Tell Jean to keep her promise.','Assess the patient’s symptoms and immediate care needs, acknowledge exhaustion and coordinate urgent support.'],answer:2,why:['Restlessness may reflect a treatable or urgent cause, and extra sedation can harm.','This imposes guilt and ignores unsafe care.','Both the patient and carer need assessment and a feasible plan.']},{q:'Which phrase best supports Jean?',options:['“You are not failing him. The situation has changed, and we need a plan that keeps you both safe. Let us arrange the right assessment and support for tonight.”','“Everyone caring for someone feels tired.”','“Hospital is always the only answer.”'],answer:0,why:['It validates the burden and links it to practical action.','This minimises her distress.','The setting should follow clinical need, wishes and what can safely be provided.']}],management:['Triage new restlessness or confusion and other acute symptoms; arrange timely assessment and emergency care where indicated.','Review medicines, symptom control and possible reversible causes. Avoid automatic extra opioids or hypnotics without assessment.','Assess the carer’s ability to cope safely tonight and involve community/palliative teams or urgent services as needed.','Explore patient wishes and capacity, feasible care settings, respite, equipment and social support. Do not interpret home-care preferences as a requirement to accept unsafe circumstances.','Agree who will act today, how to obtain out-of-hours help, a written plan and a definite follow-up for both symptom control and carer support.'],model:'“You have been carrying a great deal, and asking for help is not a failure. I want to check why he is restless and whether you can both be safe at home tonight. Let us arrange assessment and practical support, then review the wider plan together.”',trap:'A referral without a confirmed response time may not solve tonight’s problem. Clarify what will actually happen.',checks:['Assessed acute symptom causes','Reviewed medicines safely','Recognised carer limits','Coordinated support that can happen today','Agreed out-of-hours and follow-up plans'],phrases:['p7','p25','p29']}
];
const DECISIONS=[
['An adult says “no” to hospital. Does this establish incapacity?',['Yes, if the decision seems unsafe.','No. Support understanding and assess decision-specific capacity if there is reason for doubt.','Only a family member can decide.'],1,'Disagreement or an unwise choice alone does not establish incapacity. Explore barriers and the person’s understanding.'],
['A patient asks whether an urgent cancer referral means they have cancer.',['Say yes to make sure they attend.','Say it is routine and avoid mentioning cancer.','Explain that cancer is one possibility needing prompt investigation, not a confirmed diagnosis.'],2,'Honest uncertainty supports informed attendance without either alarmist certainty or false reassurance.'],
['Which safety net is most useful?',['Come back if worse.','Name relevant warning symptoms, urgency, contact route and a specific review plan.','List every complication you can remember.'],1,'The patient needs a usable decision and action, not a vague instruction or overwhelming list.'],
['A patient with asthma is repeatedly requesting SABA alone.',['Review control, acute severity and an appropriate ICS-containing treatment strategy.','Continue SABA alone if they prefer it.','Stop all inhalers until spirometry.'],0,'Frequent reliever use signals a need for review. Acute symptoms require appropriate immediate treatment; do not delay care for diagnostic testing.'],
['A PHQ-9 result is low, but the patient describes current suicidal intent.',['The score rules out a crisis.','Repeat the score next week.','Act on the clinical assessment and immediate safety needs.'],2,'Questionnaires are not tools for predicting individual suicide risk or deciding discharge.'],
['A patient takes diazepam daily and requests help to stop.',['Stop today in every case.','Discuss an individualised gradual plan and monitor withdrawal, except where exceptional medical circumstances require urgent change.','Give the same seven-day taper to everyone.'],1,'Withdrawal planning depends on the medicine, duration, dose, symptoms and person’s circumstances.'],
['A patient with dependence says they will stop alcohol tonight.',['Warn that sudden reduction can cause severe withdrawal and arrange a supported assessment.','Congratulate them and end the call.','Recommend a sleeping tablet to cover withdrawal.'],0,'Assess urgency and previous severe withdrawal. Do not supply a generic unsupervised detox plan.'],
['A new antidepressant is started for a 23-year-old. What is the usual first planned review?',['One week, with earlier action if clinical risk requires it.','One month in every case.','Only after the patient calls.'],0,'A review at one week is recommended for ages 18–25 or particular suicide concern. Immediate risk needs immediate action; the planned review is not a safety substitute.'],
['Which response best checks understanding?',['Do you understand?','Repeat my words exactly.','Could you explain the plan in your own words so I can check I explained it clearly?'],2,'Teach-back checks the clarity of the explanation and reveals misunderstandings without blaming the patient.'],
['A carer requests a treatment for an adult with a learning disability.',['Assume the carer has authority.','Include the patient, provide decision support and assess capacity for the specific decision if indicated.','Refuse all treatment.'],1,'Start from participation and the presumption of adult capacity. Legal authority must be established, not assumed.'],
['A patient has DNACPR documentation. Which statement is accurate?',['No antibiotics or hospital treatment can be offered.','It concerns CPR; other treatment decisions need their own plan.','All relatives must agree before any care.'],1,'DNACPR does not by itself mean withholding other appropriate treatment.'],
['A patient with back pain reports new bladder dysfunction and saddle numbness.',['Treat as a possible spinal emergency and arrange immediate assessment.','Wait two weeks for physiotherapy.','Assume a separate prostate problem.'],0,'These symptoms can indicate cauda equina syndrome and require immediate emergency assessment.'],
['A colleague may currently be impaired at work.',['Wait for a patient to file a formal complaint.','Promise the patient the colleague will be dismissed.','Escalate credible immediate safety concerns promptly and establish the facts.'],2,'Protecting patients is separate from the complaints process. Do not speculate or promise a disciplinary outcome.'],
['A patient misses an important follow-up twice.',['Treat this as informed refusal automatically.','Check clinical urgency, communication and access barriers, and actively close the follow-up gap.','Remove the result from the task list.'],1,'Missed contact may reflect language, transport, disability or misunderstanding. Significant findings still need a safe plan.'],
['What makes a study session active?',['Reading a model answer repeatedly.','Attempting unaided, comparing with feedback, revising and recalling after a delay.','Memorising one phrase for every patient.'],1,'Retrieval and distributed practice are useful learning approaches. Adapt the principle to different contexts rather than reciting a fixed script.'],
['What should “medical complexity” change in your plan?',['Only the number of referrals.','Nothing if the single-condition guideline is followed.','Priorities, interactions, treatment burden, feasibility and coordination.'],2,'The plan must work across the person’s conditions and circumstances, while retaining immediate safety priorities.']
].map((r,i)=>({id:'d'+(i+1),q:r[0],options:r[1],answer:r[2],why:r[3]}));
